---
interface Props {
  id?: string;
}
const { id } = Astro.props;
---

<section {id} class="py-12 sm:py-16 lg:py-20 mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6">
  <div class="mb-10 text-center">
    <p class="text-base font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent mb-2">By the numbers</p>
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">PyMCU on ATmega328P</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 max-w-2xl mx-auto text-sm">
      MicroPython and CircuitPython require a 32-bit chip with hundreds of KB of flash — they simply
      do not run on AVR. That gap is exactly why PyMCU exists: to bring Python syntax to the
      smallest hardware, compiling directly to native AVR machine code.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Flash comparison: PyMCU vs Arduino C++ -->
  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-10 mb-10">

    <!-- Blink -->
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 p-6 shadow-sm">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">
        Blink — flash usage
      </h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-500 mb-5">ATmega328P · 32 KB total flash</p>

      <div class="mb-5">
        <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline mb-1">
          <span class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">PyMCU</span>
          <span class="text-sm font-bold text-accent">124 B</span>
        </div>
        <div class="w-full bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-full h-3 overflow-hidden">
          <div class="h-3 rounded-full bg-accent" style="width: 0.4%"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline mb-1">
          <span class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">Arduino C++</span>
          <span class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-400">~924 B</span>
        </div>
        <div class="w-full bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-full h-3 overflow-hidden">
          <div class="h-3 rounded-full bg-gray-400 dark:bg-gray-500" style="width: 2.8%"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-4 text-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-500">
        Arduino's core framework adds vector table, init routines, and libc overhead even for the
        simplest sketch. PyMCU emits only what your program needs.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- DHT11 -->
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 p-6 shadow-sm">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">
        DHT11 sensor — flash usage
      </h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-500 mb-5">ATmega328P · driver + UART output</p>

      <div class="mb-5">
        <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline mb-1">
          <span class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">PyMCU</span>
          <span class="text-sm font-bold text-accent">~3 KB</span>
        </div>
        <div class="w-full bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-full h-3 overflow-hidden">
          <div class="h-3 rounded-full bg-accent" style="width: 9.4%"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline mb-1">
          <span class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">Arduino + DHT.h</span>
          <span class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-400">~5.5 KB</span>
        </div>
        <div class="w-full bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-700 rounded-full h-3 overflow-hidden">
          <div class="h-3 rounded-full bg-gray-400 dark:bg-gray-500" style="width: 17.2%"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-4 text-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-500">
        The PyMCU DHT11 driver is a Zero-Cost Abstraction written in Python — you can read the
        entire protocol implementation. No hidden C++ firmware.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- DHT transparency callout -->
  <div class="rounded-2xl border border-accent/30 bg-accent/5 dark:bg-accent/10 p-6">
    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-3">Transparency matters: reading a DHT11</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 mb-4">
      In Arduino, <code class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-700 px-1 rounded text-xs">DHT.read()</code> hides
      a timing-critical bit-bang protocol inside a compiled C++ library. It works — but the implementation
      is opaque, and the timing depends on the Arduino runtime being in a known state.
      In PyMCU, the driver is a <code class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-700 px-1 rounded text-xs">@inline</code> class
      written in Python that compiles to cycle-accurate AVR instructions. Same result, fully readable,
      deterministic by construction.
    </p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 font-mono text-xs">
      <div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-2">Arduino C++</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"><code>#include &lt;DHT.h&gt;
DHT dht(2, DHT11);
dht.begin();
// protocol hidden in library
float h = dht.readHumidity();
float t = dht.readTemperature();</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="text-accent mb-2">PyMCU</p>
        <pre class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"><code>from pymcu.boards.arduino_uno import D2
from dht11 import DHT11

sensor = DHT11(Pin(D2, Pin.IN))
sensor.measure()
# driver is pure Python you can read
print(sensor.humidity, sensor.temperature)</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
